<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chennai</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Madras</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chennai, formerly Madras, is the capital of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coromandel-coast">Coromandel Coast</a>. It grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-st-george">Fort St George</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> in 1639–1640, and became the seat of the Madras Presidency, one of the three great presidencies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Its army and fleet took part in British campaigns across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and beyond. The city was officially renamed Chennai in 1996.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chennai.jpg" alt="Chennai" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jamal haider from india · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chennai_Central.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chennai lies on the Eastern Coastal Plains, a flat stretch of clay soil on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, at an average elevation of 6.7 m. It receives most of its rain from the northeast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, and cyclones are common in that season. The name derives from Chennappa Nayaka, a Nayak ruler who served as a general under Venkata Raya of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and from whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> took the town on lease in 1639. The first official use of the name was in August 1639, in a sale deed to Francis Day of the Company.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-age">Stone Age</a> implements have been found near Pallavaram. In the 2nd century CE the region was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tondaimandalam">Tondaimandalam</a>, ruled by the Early Cholas, and the Pallavas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchi</a> ruled it independently from the 3rd to the 9th century. The Later Cholas under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aditya-i">Aditya I</a> defeated the Pallavas in 879, Jatavarman Sundara Pandyan brought the region under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> rule in 1264, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> held influence over it in the 15th century.</p><p>In 1639 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> obtained a grant of coastal land containing the fishing village of Madrasapattinam and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-st-george">Fort St George</a>, the first British fortress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Corporation of Madras was established on 29 September 1688. In the 18th century the British and the French fought for control of the city, and by the late 18th century the British had conquered most of the region and made Madras the capital of the Madras Presidency. The idea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> was conceived there after a Theosophical convention in December 1884. After 1947 Madras became capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a>, the predecessor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chennai is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, officially renamed from Madras in 1996. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 4,646,732 people within the city's 174 km2, and Tamil is the language of most residents. Its oldest surviving buildings date from the 6th to 8th centuries CE, among them the Kapaleeshwarar Temple in Mylapore and the Parthasarathy Temple in Triplicane. The Madras Music Season, begun by the Madras Music Academy in 1927, brings Carnatic music performances each December.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chennai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>English-language education, newspapers and universities founded in 1857 at Calcutta, Bombay and <b>Madras</b> created a new professional middle class.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It set up a factory at Masulipatnam in 1611, gained rights at Surat from the Mughal Emperor Jahangir in 1612, built a factory at <b>Madras</b> in 1640 and leased Bombay island in 1668; after the Anglo-Mughal War Aurangzeb let it settle at Calcutta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Khalimpur plate, the kings of the Bhojas, Matsyas, <b>Madras</b>, Kurus, Yadus, Yavanas, Avanti, Gandhara and Kira attended the ceremony and approved it, a claim that at least shows the scale of Dharmapala’s ambitions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…January 1750 Marthanda Varma dedicated the state to Padmanabha, and the kings ruled as his servants; they were conditionally promoted to Kshatriya status through sixteen mahādānams, until in 1848 Lord Dalhousie warned Uttram Tirunal that <b>Madras</b> would take over the administration if the costly practice did not end.</blockquote><a href="/empire/travancore">Travancore · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The site of <b>Madras</b>, where the English built Fort St George in 1639–40, was granted by a local nayaka who served the Aravidu kings of Vijayanagara.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars have read tamiḻ as “our own speech” or “self-speak”, and the Tamil Lexicon of the University of <b>Madras</b> defines it as “sweetness”.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
